  - UI: the list of open tabs lives in localStorage so it survives a reload. Which tab is focused lives in sessionStorage, so two browser
    windows don't switch each other.
  - Controls: ＋ opens a picker. ✕ closes a tab and asks first if Claude is still working in it. Keyboard shortcuts: ⌘W and ⌘1–9.
  - Opening another item's view (from search or the command palette) opens or focuses that item's tab instead of taking over the one
    you're in.
  - Terminal: its strip already follows switches (Panel.tsx:162-175), so it just follows the focused tab.
  - The key change: lock each session to its tab. The live nav would only say what you're looking at; it would never decide where things
    land. Context would be stored per tab, not per user.
  - Migration: one table gets re-keyed, which means a full regression run.
  - The test that matters: a session locked to tab A still writes to A while you focus tab B.

  Rough size: one block covering the UI, the server and one migration.

  One question before I go further: is a tab one item, or can you open several tabs for the same item, side by side, each with its own
  session? Both work. Several per item means locking to the tab instead of the item, which is a bit more work.

